{"timestamp": "2026-06-19T17:45:24Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27840384864", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Arnošt of Pardubice was the first Archbishop of Prague. He was also an advisor and diplomat to Emperor Charles IV.", "newValue": "Arnošt of Pardubice (1297–1364) was a pivotal ecclesiastical and political figure in 14th-century Central Europe, serving as the first Archbishop of Prague and a principal advisor to Emperor Charles IV. His elevation to the archiepiscopal see in 1344 marked a crucial step in establishing Prague as an independent metropolitan center, freeing the Bohemian church from the centuries-long ecclesiastical authority of Mainz. Born into Bohemian nobility in Hostín, his career spanned the late High Middle Ages, a period characterized by burgeoning national identity and imperial centralization within the Holy Roman Empire, with Bohemia at its heart.

Educated in Italy at Bologna and Padua, Arnošt acquired a deep knowledge of canon law, which he later applied to reform the Bohemian church. Following his appointment as Bishop of Prague in 1343, he successfully petitioned Pope Clement VI to elevate the bishopric to an archbishopric in 1344, simultaneously establishing the dioceses of Olomouc and Litomyšl as suffragans. As Archbishop, he oversaw the foundation of Charles University in Prague in 1348, a monumental achievement that transformed Bohemia into a leading intellectual hub. He also initiated the construction of St. Vitus Cathedral and was instrumental in shaping the legal framework of the Bohemian church through numerous synods.

